care = f'''<section class="page-hero page-hero--plain"><div class="wrap page-hero__inner"><div>{kicker('FIELD NOTES / TAMPA BAY')}<h1>Growing a banyan<br><em>in Tampa Bay.</em></h1><p class="lead">The climate may be warm, but “banyan” is not one tree. Match a named species to the actual space and exposure before planting.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap article-layout"><nav class="article-nav" aria-label="On this page"><span class="eyebrow">ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#identity">Botanical identity</a><a href="#climate">Climate & cold</a><a href="#space">Roots & space</a><a href="#coast">Coastal exposure</a><a href="#establish">Establishment</a></nav><article class="article-copy"><section id="identity"><span>01 / IDENTITY</span><h2>Start with the botanical name.</h2><p>Trees called banyans may be different <i>Ficus</i> species with different mature forms and ecological considerations. Ask for the scientific name on any tree under consideration. Our published listings will name the exact species offered.</p></section><section id="climate"><span>02 / CLIMATE</span><h2>Check the microclimate, not just the map.</h2><p>The USDA hardiness map describes average annual extreme minimum temperatures. It does not tell the whole story of a rare freeze, wind or salt spray at a particular address. Confirm your location on the <a href="https://planthardiness.ars.usda.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official map</a> and review the species itself.</p></section><section id="space"><span>03 / SPACE</span><h2>Give canopy and roots real room.</h2><p>Large ficus can develop extensive root systems and, for some species, aerial roots. Consider foundations, paving, pools, utilities and neighboring property before choosing the planting point. A constrained site deserves a qualified local assessment.</p></section><section id="coast"><span>04 / COAST</span><h2>Look closely at exposure.</h2><p>Distance from open water, wind, salt spray, drainage and irrigation matter. A warm zone alone does not make an exposed waterfront site suitable for every banyan.</p></section><section id="establish"><span>05 / CARE</span><h2>Establish the tree deliberately.</h2><p>Plant at the correct depth and plan irrigation around tree size, soil and rainfall while the root system establishes. A large transplanted specimen needs a site-specific aftercare plan.</p></section><div class="article-note"><strong>Sources and further reading</strong><p><a href="https://plant-directory.ifas.ufl.edu/plant-directory/ficus-benghalensis/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UF/IFAS: <i>Ficus benghalensis</i></a> · <a href="https://assessment.ifas.ufl.edu/assessments/ficus-microcarpa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UF/IFAS: <i>Ficus microcarpa</i> assessment</a> · <a href="https://ask.ifas.ufl.edu/publication/EP310" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UF/IFAS: choosing trees for a site</a></p></div></article></div></section>
{band('A QUESTION ABOUT YOUR SITE?','Begin with the space you have.','Tell us about nearby structures, exposure and the role the tree should play.','Call About Your Site')}
'''
